    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "`AcceptanceTest` is the only test class, and the full suite passes (`BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0`).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: when a create-owner request sets `sharesHousehold: true`, permit an owner at an address already used by a same-lastName owner, assign both the same stable `householdId`, and return it.\n\nThe \"allow\" half was already handled by the existing `RequireUniqueHousehold` step (it returns early when `sharesHousehold` is true). The missing half was the shared, stable `householdId` on the response. I implemented that as a **derived read-only field** rather than persisted state, which kept structural impact minimal \u2014 3 small files, none of them large classes:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added `householdId` (read-only string) to the `Owner` response schema.\n2. **`HouseholdId.java`** (new, tiny) \u2014 `of(lastName, address)` computes a stable identifier from the normalised lastName+address (same case/whitespace normalisation as the duplicate check), so any two co-resident same-lastName owners deterministically resolve to the same value.\n3. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 one added `@Mapping` expression wiring `householdId` into `OwnerDto`.\n\nBecause the id is derived deterministically from the fields the household is keyed on, owners `b` and `c` (same lastName+address as `a`) get the identical, non-blank `householdId` automatically \u2014 visible on both the POST response and `GET /api/owners/{id}` \u2014 with no entity, database schema, persistence, or pipeline-step changes, and no edits to any high-complexity class (`Owner`, the POST steps).",
